? An RPN calculator over 16-bit numbers held one digit per tape.
?
? Third in a family. rpn.tmc stores a value as a variable-length binary number
? in the standard library's delimited form; rpnhex.tmc packs four hex digits
? into four consecutive cells of one tape; this one spreads those same four
? digits across FOUR TAPES, one digit position each, cellA the most
? significant through cellD the least.
?
? A stack entry is therefore one cell on each of the four tapes, and the four
? heads move in lockstep. Everything that was a walk becomes a single move:
?
?   The head index IS the stack depth. Home is index 0 and holds nothing; the
?   entry at depth d sits at index d. Push is one move right, pop one move
?   left, and "are there two entries" is one move left, one read, one move
?   back — on a single tape, since the other three track it.
?
?   That test rests on one invariant no single rule states: INDEX 0 IS NEVER
?   WRITTEN. `pushTok` moves right before it writes, so the first entry lands
?   at index 1; `popTop` blanks the cell it leaves; and `setZero` writes at
?   the current head, which is the depth index and is only ever reached at
?   depth 1 or more. A rule that wrote home would make a blank read there stop
?   meaning "empty" and break every depth test in the file at once.
?
?   Setting a number to zero is one rule and one step: the four digits are
?   under four heads at once, so one write vector clears all of them.
?
?   Adding is four steps, one per digit position, with no head movement at
?   all and nothing to rewind afterwards. The carry rides a separate one-cell
?   tape from each digit's rule to the next.
?
? Adding is flat and cheap: `0005 0003 +` is 166 steps, `1234 0FFE +` is 164,
? `FFFF 0001 -` is 166. That is the fewest of the four files, and
? rpnreg.tmc's header carries the comparison against the other three.
?
? The cost is in plain sight and is the reason to read the files together:
? fourteen tapes means every rule of the machine world is a fourteen-cell
? vector, and the adder that was one 512-row rule on rpnhex.tmc is four of
? them here, one per digit position. The layout that makes the stack free
? makes the source wide and the image four times larger. Multiply and divide
? are still counting loops, so their cost still scales with a value:
? `00FF 0010 *` is 1,039 steps while `0000 1234 *` is 251,522.
?
? Head conventions. All four stack heads sit at the same index at all times,
? and that index is the depth: 0 when empty, d when d entries are on the
? stack. The work registers `w` and `v` are four fixed cells under four heads
? that never move. `flag` is a single cell holding 0 or 1 that never moves.
?
? Errors. `flag` carries the carry or borrow out of the top digit and the
? CALLER decides what it means — an operator treats it as overflow or
? underflow and halts, the multiply and divide loops treat it as their loop
? condition — so no routine here halts on its own. Input faults halt; an
? internal invariant broken by a bug in this file traps instead, and the
? difference in the exit code is deliberate (docs/tmt/cli.md (exit codes)).
?
? Input format. Tokens on `expr`, separated by blanks, terminated by '#'. A
? literal is exactly four hex digits — no more, no fewer — and an operator is
? one of '+', '-', '*', '/'. So `0005 0003 + 0002 *` is (5+3)*2. The sentinel
? is not optional: without it the scanner walks right over blank tape forever
? and the run ends on the step budget, as a trap rather than a halt.
?
? Eight lint findings are expected and correct: each of the eight digit rules
? in the two two-operand ripples expands to 512 match rows, over the 256-row
? `binding-product-threshold` cutoff (docs/tmt/lint.md). Sixteen digit pairs
? by sixteen by two carry-in values is what a hex adder IS — and here that
? table exists once per digit position instead of once per adder.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Alphabets
//
// The digits are NUMERIC literals, not glyphs, because a fold is numeric-only
// and an operator applied to a glyph binding is the `char-arithmetic` error
// (docs/tmt/language.md (substitution)).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

? Blank plus the sixteen hex digits. A digit's glyph label is its value, which
? is what lets a fold's result name a symbol.
alphabet hex { '_', 0..15 }

? Expression tokens: hex digits, the four operators, and the '#' sentinel that
? ends the expression.
alphabet tok { '_', 0..15, '+', '-', '*', '/', '#' }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The stack, in one graph
//
// Compare this with the same test in rpnhex.tmc, where entries are four cells
// apart on one tape and the walk is eight moves through eleven states. Here
// the entry below the top is one cell left, so the test is two moves — and it
// touches only the most significant tape, because the other three track it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

? Leave through `two` when the stack holds at least two entries and through
? `fewer` otherwise. Entry head at the depth index; both exits restore it.
graph hasTwoGraph(tape n: hex, state two, state fewer) {
  entry state top {
    [0..15] -> move [<] goto look;
    [*]     -> fewer; // blank: the stack is empty
  }
  state look {
    [0..15] -> move [>] two;
    [*]     -> move [>] fewer;
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Digit arithmetic
//
// Four digits sit under four heads, so a ripple is four rules and no motion.
// Each binds one digit position of each operand plus the carry-in, and writes
// that position's result digit and the carry-out; the flag cell is what
// carries a value from one rule to the next, since a rule cannot branch on
// what its own fold computed.
//
// The carry-out fold is the part worth reading twice. The fold grammar has
// `+ - * %` and no division (docs/tmt/language.md (substitution)), so a sum s
// in 0..31 cannot be shifted right by four the obvious way. But `s - s % 16`
// is 0 below sixteen and exactly 16 at or above it, and `16 % 15` is 1, so
// `(s - s % 16) % 15` IS the carry. Subtraction runs the same trick upside
// down: `1 - (…)` turns "no borrow" into "borrow".
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

? n := 0. One rule, one step: every digit is under a head already.
routine setZero(tape nA: hex, tape nB: hex, tape nC: hex, tape nD: hex) {
  entry state clear { [*, *, *, *] -> write [0, 0, 0, 0] return; }
}

? n := n + 1. Leaves the carry out of the top digit in `flag`.
routine incr(
  tape nA: hex,
  tape nB: hex,
  tape nC: hex,
  tape nD: hex,
  tape flag: hex
) {
  entry state seed { [*, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, 1] goto digitD; }
  state digitD {
    [*, *, *, 0..15 as x, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, -, {(x+k)%16}, {((x+k)-((x+k)%16))%15}] goto digitC;
  }
  state digitC {
    [*, *, 0..15 as x, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, {(x+k)%16}, -, {((x+k)-((x+k)%16))%15}] goto digitB;
  }
  state digitB {
    [*, 0..15 as x, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, {(x+k)%16}, -, -, {((x+k)-((x+k)%16))%15}] goto digitA;
  }
  state digitA {
    [0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [{(x+k)%16}, -, -, -, {((x+k)-((x+k)%16))%15}] return;
  }
}

? n := n - 1. Leaves the borrow out of the top digit in `flag`, which is also
? how a caller learns that `n` was zero.
routine decr(
  tape nA: hex,
  tape nB: hex,
  tape nC: hex,
  tape nD: hex,
  tape flag: hex
) {
  entry state seed { [*, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, 1] goto digitD; }
  state digitD {
    [*, *, *, 0..15 as x, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, -, {(x-k+16)%16}, {1-(((x-k+16)-((x-k+16)%16))%15)}] goto digitC;
  }
  state digitC {
    [*, *, 0..15 as x, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, {(x-k+16)%16}, -, {1-(((x-k+16)-((x-k+16)%16))%15)}] goto digitB;
  }
  state digitB {
    [*, 0..15 as x, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, {(x-k+16)%16}, -, -, {1-(((x-k+16)-((x-k+16)%16))%15)}] goto digitA;
  }
  state digitA {
    [0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [{(x-k+16)%16}, -, -, -, {1-(((x-k+16)-((x-k+16)%16))%15)}] return;
  }
}

? acc := acc + add. `add` is unchanged; the carry out of the top digit is left
? in `flag`.
routine addInto(
  tape accA: hex,
  tape accB: hex,
  tape accC: hex,
  tape accD: hex,
  tape addA: hex,
  tape addB: hex,
  tape addC: hex,
  tape addD: hex,
  tape flag: hex
) {
  entry state seed {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, 0] goto digitD;
  }
  state digitD {
    [*, *, *, 0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, -, {(x+y+k)%16}, -, -, -, -, {((x+y+k)-((x+y+k)%16))%15}] goto digitC;
  }
  state digitC {
    [*, *, 0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, {(x+y+k)%16}, -, -, -, -, -, {((x+y+k)-((x+y+k)%16))%15}] goto digitB;
  }
  state digitB {
    [*, 0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, {(x+y+k)%16}, -, -, -, -, -, -, {((x+y+k)-((x+y+k)%16))%15}] goto digitA;
  }
  state digitA {
    [0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, *, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [{(x+y+k)%16}, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, {((x+y+k)-((x+y+k)%16))%15}] return;
  }
}

? acc := acc - sub. `sub` is unchanged; the borrow out of the top digit is
? left in `flag`, and on borrow `acc` holds the result modulo 65536.
routine subInto(
  tape accA: hex,
  tape accB: hex,
  tape accC: hex,
  tape accD: hex,
  tape subA: hex,
  tape subB: hex,
  tape subC: hex,
  tape subD: hex,
  tape flag: hex
) {
  entry state seed {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, 0] goto digitD;
  }
  state digitD {
    [*, *, *, 0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, -, {(x-y-k+16)%16}, -, -, -, -, {1-(((x-y-k+16)-((x-y-k+16)%16))%15)}] goto digitC;
  }
  state digitC {
    [*, *, 0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, -, {(x-y-k+16)%16}, -, -, -, -, -, {1-(((x-y-k+16)-((x-y-k+16)%16))%15)}] goto digitB;
  }
  state digitB {
    [*, 0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [-, {(x-y-k+16)%16}, -, -, -, -, -, -, {1-(((x-y-k+16)-((x-y-k+16)%16))%15)}] goto digitA;
  }
  state digitA {
    [0..15 as x, *, *, *, 0..15 as y, *, *, *, 0..1 as k] -> write [{(x-y-k+16)%16}, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, {1-(((x-y-k+16)-((x-y-k+16)%16))%15)}] return;
  }
}

? dst := src. Four rules, one per digit position; nothing moves.
routine copyNum(
  tape srcA: hex,
  tape srcB: hex,
  tape srcC: hex,
  tape srcD: hex,
  tape dstA: hex,
  tape dstB: hex,
  tape dstC: hex,
  tape dstD: hex
) {
  entry state digitA {
    [0..15 as d, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, {d}, -, -, -] goto digitB;
  }
  state digitB {
    [*, 0..15 as d, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, -, {d}, -, -] goto digitC;
  }
  state digitC {
    [*, *, 0..15 as d, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, -, -, {d}, -] goto digitD;
  }
  state digitD {
    [*, *, *, 0..15 as d, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, -, -, -, {d}] return;
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The stack proper
//
// `pushTok` reads the expression and validates it, so a malformed literal
// halts. `popTop` moves a number this file itself produced; its sweep carries
// no catch-all, so a violated head convention traps rather than being
// reported as an input fault.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

? Push the four-digit literal `src`'s head is sitting on. Halts if the literal
? is not exactly four digits long. On exit `src`'s head is on the cell after
? the literal — which is where the scanner wants it.
routine pushTok(
  tape sA: hex,
  tape sB: hex,
  tape sC: hex,
  tape sD: hex,
  tape src: tok
) {
  // One move right on all four heads and the entry exists; there is nothing
  // to position and no separator to leave.
  entry state open { [*, *, *, *, *] -> move [>, >, >, >, .] goto takeA; }
  // The binding is read on `src` and written on a stack tape: a passthrough
  // substitution names a glyph, and both alphabets carry the sixteen digits.
  state takeA {
    [*, *, *, *, 0..15 as d] -> write [{d}, -, -, -, -] move [., ., ., ., >] goto takeB;
    [*, *, *, *, *]          -> halt;
  }
  state takeB {
    [*, *, *, *, 0..15 as d] -> write [-, {d}, -, -, -] move [., ., ., ., >] goto takeC;
    [*, *, *, *, *]          -> halt;
  }
  state takeC {
    [*, *, *, *, 0..15 as d] -> write [-, -, {d}, -, -] move [., ., ., ., >] goto takeD;
    [*, *, *, *, *]          -> halt;
  }
  state takeD {
    [*, *, *, *, 0..15 as d] -> write [-, -, -, {d}, -] move [., ., ., ., >] goto tail;
    [*, *, *, *, *]          -> halt;
  }
  state tail {
    [*, *, *, *, 0..15] -> halt; // a fifth digit: the literal is too long
    [*, *, *, *, *]     -> return;
  }
}

? Move the top entry off the stack and into `dst`. The stack must not be
? empty. On exit the four stack heads are one cell left — which is the new
? depth — and the cells they left are blank.
routine popTop(
  tape sA: hex,
  tape sB: hex,
  tape sC: hex,
  tape sD: hex,
  tape dA: hex,
  tape dB: hex,
  tape dC: hex,
  tape dD: hex
) {
  entry state digitA {
    [0..15 as d, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> write ['_', -, -, -, {d}, -, -, -] goto digitB;
  }
  state digitB {
    [*, 0..15 as d, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, '_', -, -, -, {d}, -, -] goto digitC;
  }
  state digitC {
    [*, *, 0..15 as d, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, '_', -, -, -, {d}, -] goto digitD;
  }
  state digitD {
    [*, *, *, 0..15 as d, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, '_', -, -, -, {d}] goto drop;
  }
  state drop {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> move [<, <, <, <, ., ., ., .] return;
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The machine
//
// Fourteen tapes: the expression, four for the stack, four each for the two
// work registers, and the flag. Every rule below carries a fourteen-cell
// vector, and almost all of those cells are wildcards — the price of the
// layout, paid once per line.
//
// The result of an operation is left where the left operand already was, on
// the stack top, so `+`, `-` and `*` never push anything back.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

machine {
  tape expr: tok; // read once, left to right

  tape sA: hex;
  tape sB: hex;
  tape sC: hex;
  tape sD: hex; // the stack
  tape wA: hex;
  tape wB: hex;
  tape wC: hex;
  tape wD: hex; // right operand
  tape vA: hex;
  tape vB: hex;
  tape vC: hex;
  tape vD: hex; // second work register

  tape flag: hex; // carry / borrow

  // The flag cell is read by every ripple, so it has to hold a digit before
  // the first one runs; a blank there would match no row and trap.
  entry state init {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> write [-, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, -, 0] goto scan;
  }

  ? The token scanner. Blanks separate tokens and are skipped; an operator is
  ? not consumed here, because the operands have to be popped before the
  ? machine can know which operation to run.
  state scan {
    [0..15, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call pushTok(
                                                        sA = sA,
                                                        sB = sB,
                                                        sC = sC,
                                                        sD = sD,
                                                        src = expr
                                                      ) then scan;
    ['_', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *]   -> move [>, ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., .] goto scan;
    ['+', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *]   -> goto needTwo;
    ['-', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *]   -> goto needTwo;
    ['*', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *]   -> goto needTwo;
    ['/', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *]   -> goto needTwo;
    ['#', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *]   -> goto atEnd;
  }

  // The depth test needs only the most significant stack tape: the other
  // three heads are at the same index by construction.
  graft hasTwoGraph(n = sA, two = popRight, fewer = underflow) as needTwo;

  ? Pop the right operand into `w`. The left one stays where it is, on the
  ? stack top, and every operation below writes its result there.
  state popRight {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call popTop(
                                                    sA = sA,
                                                    sB = sB,
                                                    sC = sC,
                                                    sD = sD,
                                                    dA = wA,
                                                    dB = wB,
                                                    dC = wC,
                                                    dD = wD
                                                  ) then apply;
  }

  ? The right operand is in `w` and the left is under the stack heads; consume
  ? the operator and run it.
  state apply {
    ['+', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> move [>, ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., .] goto opAdd;
    ['-', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> move [>, ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., .] goto opSub;
    ['*', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> move [>, ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., .] goto mulSave;
    ['/', *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> move [>, ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., ., .] goto divGuard;
  }

  state opAdd {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call addInto(
                                                    accA = sA,
                                                    accB = sB,
                                                    accC = sC,
                                                    accD = sD,
                                                    addA = wA,
                                                    addB = wB,
                                                    addC = wC,
                                                    addD = wD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then addTest;
  }
  state addTest {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, 0] -> goto scan;
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> halt; // over 16 bits
  }

  state opSub {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call subInto(
                                                    accA = sA,
                                                    accB = sB,
                                                    accC = sC,
                                                    accD = sD,
                                                    subA = wA,
                                                    subB = wB,
                                                    subC = wC,
                                                    subD = wD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then subTest;
  }
  state subTest {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, 0] -> goto scan;
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> halt; // below zero
  }

  // Multiply: move the left operand into `v`, turn the stack top into the
  // accumulator, then add `v` in once per unit of `w`. The borrow out of
  // `decr` is the loop's only termination test — no zero test on `w` is
  // needed, or written.
  state mulSave {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call copyNum(
                                                    srcA = sA,
                                                    srcB = sB,
                                                    srcC = sC,
                                                    srcD = sD,
                                                    dstA = vA,
                                                    dstB = vB,
                                                    dstC = vC,
                                                    dstD = vD
                                                  ) then mulInit;
  }
  state mulInit {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call setZero(
                                                    nA = sA,
                                                    nB = sB,
                                                    nC = sC,
                                                    nD = sD
                                                  ) then mulStep;
  }
  state mulStep {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call decr(
                                                    nA = wA,
                                                    nB = wB,
                                                    nC = wC,
                                                    nD = wD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then mulTest;
  }
  state mulTest {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, 0] -> goto mulAdd; // `w` had something left
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> goto scan;   // `w` ran out
  }
  state mulAdd {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call addInto(
                                                    accA = sA,
                                                    accB = sB,
                                                    accC = sC,
                                                    accD = sD,
                                                    addA = vA,
                                                    addB = vB,
                                                    addC = vC,
                                                    addD = vD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then mulTestCarry;
  }
  state mulTestCarry {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, 0] -> goto mulStep;
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> halt; // the product does not fit
  }

  // Divide: count how many times `w` comes out of the stack top, in `v`, then
  // move the count back onto the stack. A zero divisor would never borrow and
  // the loop would never end, so it is ruled out first — by decrementing `w`
  // and reading the borrow, the same trick the multiply loop uses.
  state divGuard {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call decr(
                                                    nA = wA,
                                                    nB = wB,
                                                    nC = wC,
                                                    nD = wD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then divTestZero;
  }
  state divTestZero {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, 0] -> goto divRestore;
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> halt; // division by zero
  }
  state divRestore {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call incr(
                                                    nA = wA,
                                                    nB = wB,
                                                    nC = wC,
                                                    nD = wD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then divInit;
  }
  state divInit {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call setZero(
                                                    nA = vA,
                                                    nB = vB,
                                                    nC = vC,
                                                    nD = vD
                                                  ) then divStep;
  }
  state divStep {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call subInto(
                                                    accA = sA,
                                                    accB = sB,
                                                    accC = sC,
                                                    accD = sD,
                                                    subA = wA,
                                                    subB = wB,
                                                    subC = wC,
                                                    subD = wD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then divTest;
  }
  state divTest {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, 0] -> goto divBump;  // it came out whole again
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> goto divStore; // it did not
  }
  state divBump {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call incr(
                                                    nA = vA,
                                                    nB = vB,
                                                    nC = vC,
                                                    nD = vD,
                                                    flag = flag
                                                  ) then divStep;
  }
  state divStore {
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> call copyNum(
                                                    srcA = vA,
                                                    srcB = vB,
                                                    srcC = vC,
                                                    srcD = vD,
                                                    dstA = sA,
                                                    dstB = sB,
                                                    dstC = sC,
                                                    dstD = sD
                                                  ) then scan;
  }

  // A well-formed expression leaves exactly one value. Two or more means the
  // input was several expressions; none means it was empty.
  graft hasTwoGraph(n = sA, two = tooMany, fewer = maybeOne) as atEnd;
  state maybeOne {
    [*, 0..15, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> stop;
    [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *]     -> halt;
  }

  state underflow { [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> halt; }
  state tooMany   { [*, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *, *] -> halt; }
}
